WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.769
What we're watching here is

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.599
a government funding

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.459
of a militarization of the

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.809
police.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.819
A door swung open in the late

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:10.820
sixties.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.019
Nothing that big or bright

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:19.949
had ever happened.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.899
And in so many American cities.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.929
And someone, something

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.030
sprang up and slammed it shut.

00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:33.869
Riotsville, USA a new documentary by

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.789
filmmaker Sierra Pettengill, is

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.439
a surrealist look at the dawn of

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.959
police militarization in America.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.519
Following the death of Michael Brown

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.529
in Ferguson, Missouri, Americans

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.059
became increasingly aware of the

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.889
growing use of military tools

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.839
and tactics by law enforcement.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.279
But as Pettengill's film makes

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.409
clear, the practice began decades

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.449
earlier in the 1960s after

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.519
protests erupted in cities across

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.689
the country.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.699
In 1967, President Lyndon

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.229
Johnson appointed the Kerner

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.029
Commission to study what had caused

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.009
a series of urban riots that led

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.199
to deadly clashes between protesters

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.609
and police.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.229
The commission's report recommended

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.939
an ambitious set of initiatives to

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.769
combat racism in America

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.239
and a minor measure calling for

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.799
increased funding and training for

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.669
police.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.469
It was that minor recommendation

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.029
that President Johnson latched on

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:21.749
to.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.369
One conclusion is that the police in

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.949
the military fell, people were shot,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.139
people died, that strategically

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.059
there was a need for reform.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.060
And so Riotsville

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.809
in ways that are very important for

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.369
me, in how I think about the present

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.439
tense is a really direct

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.239
example of what police reform looks

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.260
like in practice.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.229
Riotsville was

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.749
a series of fake towns

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.609
that looked like the low budget film

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.169
sets that were constructed on

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.059
military bases in

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.159
the late sixties for

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.499
training in civil disturbance

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.789
control.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.799
People ranging from FBI agents

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.869
to governors,

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.119
rank and file police officers,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.009
police chiefs were all brought

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.529
in for this course and Riotsville

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.599
was a sort of day long staged

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.600
reenactment.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.649
What they're expecting when they go

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.169
there is to learn about coordination

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.909
between departments, how

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.159
communication should be happening.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.989
They are learning how

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.689
a riot typically unfolds,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.839
which is an invention and a creation

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.579
of the military and the government

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.559
that sort of reinforces itself.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.649
We need a new school board for this

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:37.650
town. Right.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.249
That thing looks like it took Henry

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:41.250
Ford to school

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:43.999
and playgrounds.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.559
We haven't got one playground in

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.560
this town, do we?

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.179
Isolated and secured

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.829
from outside interference or

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:53.029
agitation.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.889
Such demonstrations will usually

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:56.869
terminate peacefully in a minimum

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.249
of time.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.139
Now an incident will demand more

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.639
positive action.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.139
They don't tell me what to do, man.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:03.919
I'm a civilian.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.809
You doing my time and

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.519
that army. You know, in these stage

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.379
demonstrations, the men dressed

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.449
in black will typify hardcore

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:12.770
professional agitators.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.099
When the group refuses to disband.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.409
Action must be taken.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.089
Now the agitators shift their

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.249
troublemaking skills into high gear,

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.079
using the naturally contagious

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.389
qualities of excitement and violence

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.429
to incite them into action.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.589
It's largely the first time that

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.329
the federal government is funding

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.849
local police departments directly.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.069
Federal allocation for local police

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.429
went from $0 in 1964

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.079
to 10 million in 65,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.299
and then by 1970 was 300

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.979
million.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.689
The same rhetoric for police reform

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:49.519
now, which is you can

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.169
you can pull a lot of things under

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.419
the rubric of how to reduce harm,

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.069
but here's a tank just in case.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.139
You know, what the justifications

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.819
are for grant writing purposes

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.349
versus what's actually being

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.239
experienced on the ground are

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.129
often at odds.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.249
A great deal of the archival footage

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:08.689
used in the film was shot by the

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:09.709
military.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.689
Pettengill, an archival researcher

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.219
was particularly drawn to the

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.299
government films.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.549
Finding a visual record

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.439
of the fantasy space

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:19.699
of what the government and the

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.700
military,

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.369
how they are designing the world.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.919
You know, they're building their own

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.569
city and they're populating it and

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.819
they're scripting the actions.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.409
And I feel like that's a very rare

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.569
thing to come across as a

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.489
sort of visual representation of

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.109
of what they think, how they

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.969
conceive of the world.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.009
When I saw the footage, the first

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.749
thing I was struck by was this

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.479
simultaneous, deep absurdity

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.889
and stupidity.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.989
It's really dumb and bumbling and

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:49.219
and and bad.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:51.289
And you do want to laugh

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.379
at parts. And then extreme,

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.509
pretty chilling violence.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:58.339
And the pairing of those two

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.929
things does feel like

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.989
what it feels like to be living in

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.029
in America in many ways.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.589
And that was a real guiding force in

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.439
the edit room was

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.239
how do you make this absurdity

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.399
reflective of the callousness

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.859
and lack of care and attention

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.169
on the part of the military

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:19.429
to government?

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:20.899
The film takes pains to make the

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.849
viewer aware of its manipulations

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.739
with jump cuts, digital artifacts

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.509
and jarring editing choices.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:28.189
The goal is to drive home that what

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:29.749
you're seeing, like Riotsville

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.699
itself, is constructed.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.099
The idea of being in an immersive

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.749
documentary where you feel like

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:37.099
you're right there and you're in it

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.719
and you're living through it, is not

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.639
actually an

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.499
accurate way of experiencing

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.389
things. And to me, it's

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:45.859
very important that we are watching

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.059
this for a reason, which is the

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.409
reason we keep asking What are we

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.729
looking at? What do we make of this?

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:50.839
What do we make of this in the

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:52.849
future it was meant to insure?

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.829
And so not hiding the

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.809
digital nature of things and and

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.879
in some ways trying to probe as far

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.529
in as you can get and see what's

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.239
there and see the limitations of the

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.240
pixels.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.709
The film is pretty self-aware

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.629
in its construction, and one

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.099
of the most important political

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.599
points to me and the film is that

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.579
this is all constructed, that we do

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:14.869
not live in a historical

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:15.799
inevitability.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.329
We are not forced to live in a

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.249
really repressive, brutal state.

